Batteries
=====

They'd be like scrolls of charging, except they charge more, and only 
work on electric items, like metal detectors and chainsaws.

Chainsaws
=====

They'd be weapons generated at around (0:20), and each time you hit with 
it, it does massive damage and loses one charge. If you try to hit with 
it while it doesn't have any charges, you simply bash the monster, doing 
very little damage. It would be charged with PYO, scrolls of charging, 
or batteries.

Guns
=====

I know Slash'EM already implements them, but their implementation isn't 
very good.

I think guns should be more like wands than bows. There would be only a 
few guns, say, pistols, rifles, and six-shooters. They would either be 
"loaded" or "nonloaded", an attribute that have to be identified (either 
by trying to load it, trying to fire it, or reading scroll of-/casting 
spell of-/paying a shopkeeper in Slash'EM to Identify). Except for 
six-shooters, which would be the standard chargable item "six-shooter 
(0:6)" or something like that.

Firing guns would have to be different. Having to z)ap or a)pply guns 
would be very cumbersome if you're planning to use a gun as a main 
weapon. So, instead, you just have to fight with it. If you try to hit a 
monster with a loaded gun wielded, you'll shoot in that direction, 
hitting the monster. I know what you're thinking - "Guns are ranged 
weapons, how do I shoot from a distance?" By using F)ight, of course. 
F)ighting an empty space with a gun will cause you to shoot in that 
direction. If the gun isn't loaded, however, you'll simply bash the 
monster (or air, if there's no monster there), doing barely any damage. 
Pistols and six-shooters would have a short range, rifles would have 
longer ranges.

The guns would be charged by bullet cartridges, which would also be 
chargeable ("bullet cartridge (0:12)"). a)pplying a bullet cartrige will 
charge guns, and take 1 charge for each gun it charges (except 
six-shooters, which it would, obviously, take six charges). This would 
take 1 turn per gun (even six-shooters, since you load them all at 
once). Of course, it would do nothing once it's out of charges. There 
would also be an "Autoload" option. When Autoload is True, if you fire a 
gun, it has zero charges after that if it is a six-shooter, and you have 
bullet cartridges with nonzero charges, you will automatically load the 
gun (taking one turn doing it). Either way, if you try to fire a gun 
identified as "nonloaded" and you have bullet cartridges with nonzero 
charges, you automatically load it before firing.

